{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.CircleIntegralFinsetSum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.CircleIntegralFinsetSum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.CircleIntegral","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Divisor","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Analytic.IsolatedZeros","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.FactorizedRational"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.analyticOrderAt_eq_of_finset_factorisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.analyticOrderAt_eq_of_finset_factorisation","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L193-L223","doc":"**In a `Finset` factorisation the exponent at a factor is the order of\nvanishing there.**\n\nSplitting `(· − a)^{m a}` off the product leaves an analytic function that does\nnot vanish at `a` — the two remaining kinds of factor, `(· − b)^{m b}` for\n`b ≠ a` and the cofactor `g`, are both nonzero there — and that is exactly what\n`analyticOrderAt f a = m a` says. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.exists_finset_factorisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.exists_finset_factorisation","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L132-L191","doc":"**An analytic function factorises over its zeros on a relatively compact,\nconnected open set.**\n\n`extract_zeros_poles` gives this modulo a codiscrete set and in `finprod`/`zpow`\nform; the identity theorem upgrades the equality and\n`finprod_zpow_eq_finset_prod_npow` normalises the shape.\n\nThe multiplicities are strictly positive and `S ⊆ V`, so `S` is *exactly* the\nzero set of `f` on `V` — without those, `a ∈ S` would carry no information and\nthe factorisation could not be read backwards. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.finprod_zpow_eq_finset_prod_npow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.finprod_zpow_eq_finset_prod_npow","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L113-L128","doc":"A `finprod` of `zpow` factors with a non-negative, finitely-supported\nexponent is a `Finset.prod` of `npow` factors. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.codiscreteWithin_neBot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.codiscreteWithin_neBot","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L87-L105","doc":"For a nonempty open `U ⊆ ℂ`, the codiscrete-within filter is non-trivial.\nWithout this the lemma above is vacuous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.eqOn_of_eventuallyEq_codiscreteWithin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.eqOn_of_eventuallyEq_codiscreteWithin","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L71-L79","doc":"Analytic functions agreeing codiscretely on a preconnected set agree on it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.divisor_support_finite_of_subset","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.divisor_support_finite_of_subset","kind":"theorem","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L49-L65","doc":"If `f` is meromorphic on a compact `U` and `V ⊆ U`, the divisor of `f` on `V`\nhas finite support.\n\nThe support is discrete (isolated zeros) and closed in `U`; a discrete closed\nsubset of a compact set is finite. `divisor_restrict` transports that from `U`\nto `V`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.integral_fun_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.integral_fun_sum","kind":"theorem","line":34,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/CircleIntegralFinsetSum.lean#L34-L42","doc":"Linearity of the circle integral over a `Finset` sum.\n\nMirrors Mathlib's own proof: unfold `circleIntegral` and apply\n`intervalIntegral.integral_finset_sum`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}